   "title": "FALSE HORIZONS Netanyahus Far Right Consensus Sweeps Israeli Election as the Facade of Democracy Crumbles",
   "summary": "Benjamin Netanyahu boastfully declared to his base: \"We are going to fulfill our promise that there will be no Palestinian state; this place belongs to us entirely.\"",
   "image": "images/1793564993512-Netanyahu-gadi.jpg",
   "url": "",
   "slug": "false-horizons-netanyahus-far-right-consensus-sweeps-israeli-election-as-the-facade-of-democracy-crumbles",
   "body": "JERUSALEM — Israel’s political landscape finalized its terrifying, predictable descent into hyper-nationalism as official final results confirmed a sweeping victory for Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and his extremist, fascist-adjacent coalition partners. In the nation’s national elections, the electorate shattered voter participation records. However, international analysts and rights groups warn that this high turnout was not a celebration of democratic liberty, but a final, absolute consolidation of a hyper-militarized regime dedicated to permanent apartheid and colonial expansion.<p></p><div><br></div><div>Total Right Wing Domination by the Numbers</div><div><br></div><div>The final tallies released by the Central Elections Committee confirmed a historic and crushing victory for the nationalist right. Benjamin Netanyahu’s Likud bloc, tightly bound to ultra-nationalist religious factions, successfully captured a decisive 64 seats in the 120-seat Knesset, completely erasing any hopes of a center-right upset. Gadi Eisenkot’s newly formed Yashar party collapsed under the weight of the right-wing surge, securing a meager 14 seats, while the traditional political left was entirely wiped off the parliamentary map.</div><div>Voter turnout concluded at a staggering 72.4% of eligible voters, the highest national engagement recorded in over three decades. Yet, international observers noted that the record turnout was fueled by a relentless campaign of anti-Arab incitement. Throughout polling day, extreme far-right figures like National Security Minister Itamar Ben-Gvir openly deployed vigilante groups to Palestinian towns and Arab communities within Israel, executing systematic voter suppression tactics under the guise of security monitoring.</div><div><br></div><div>The Illusion of the Zionist Majority and the Arab Kingmakers</div><div><br></div><div>For decades, the Zionist majority in the Knesset has systematically ignored, marginalized, and excluded the Arab-led parties, treating them as permanent backbenchers unfit to ever sit in a governing coalition. Throughout this campaign, both Netanyahu and his challenger Eisenkot routinely reassured Jewish voters that they would never rely on Arab lawmakers to form a majority, maintaining a long-standing political taboo that effectively renders the democratic rights of Palestinian citizens of Israel second-class.</div><div>Yet, the final election data has exposed the absolute fragility of that Zionist consensus by turning the Arab parties into the ultimate, ironic kingmakers of this election. In a stunning display of political resilience against aggressive right-wing suppression, the unified Arab factions surged past the electoral threshold to secure a vital bloc of seats. Because Netanyahu's coalition holds exactly 64 seats, any future structural instability, internal rebellion from extremist fringe elements, or legislative gridlock places the Arab parties in a position of supreme tactical leverage. The very factions the Zionist establishment tried to write out of existence have emerged as the only mathematical force capable of blocking total legislative impunity, holding the real balance of power over the survival of the government.</div><div><br></div><div>Two Contenders, One Right Wing Agenda</div><div><br></div><div>While western corporate media attempted to frame this election as a dramatic, polarized choice between Netanyahu and Eisenkot, experts from the International Crisis Group repeatedly stressed that the ideological difference between the two men on core issues was entirely non-existent. Both platforms launched their campaigns by explicitly rejecting Palestinian self-determination, promising permanent military subjugation, and championing the illegal expansion of Jewish settlements across the occupied West Bank.</div><div>The fundamental ideological synchronization was explicitly laid out in the candidates' own declarations to media outlets like Al Jazeera:</div><div>• Benjamin Netanyahu boastfully declared to his base: \"We are going to fulfill our promise that there will be no Palestinian state; this place belongs to us entirely.\"</div><div>• Gadi Eisenkot, far from offering an alternative, aggressively echoed this sentiment, telling reporters: \"My position on the Palestinian issue has remained consistent: The principle of two states for two peoples is a dangerous mistake.\"</div><div><br></div><div>Aggression Abroad, Fascism at Home</div><div><br></div><div>Netanyahu’s extended tenure has successfully plunged the wider Middle East into cataclysmic regional warfare, orchestrating devastating military operations across Gaza, Lebanon, Syria, and Iran. But as The Associated Press and The New Arab reported, the catastrophic humanitarian devastation and subsequent international war crimes warrants generated zero moral friction within the Israeli electorate.</div><div>Instead, the aggressive campaigns enjoyed absolute, near-universal domestic adoration. With the final results cementing the kingmaker status of radical extremists Bezalel Smotrich and Itamar Ben-Gvir — who have openly advocated for the total forced expulsion of Palestinians — the domestic society has fully embraced a hyper-militarized consensus. The political center has not just shrunk; it has willingly dissolved into fascist-adjacent uniformity.</div><div><br></div><div>A Bleak Reality for Occupied Palestine</div><div><br></div><div>For the millions of trapped Palestinians living under total military blockade and military occupation who have no voice, no vote, and no rights in this sham democracy, the final election numbers seal a brutal fate.</div><div>The immediate outlook under this consolidated 64-seat right-wing majority points toward an unprecedented escalation of radical settler violence, rapid land theft, and the formal annexation of the West Bank. International media coverage ultimately confirms that regardless of which right-wing warlord sat at the top of the ballot, the structural alienation, violent subjugation, and systemic displacement of the Palestinian people remains the permanent, unyielding policy of the Israeli state.</div>",
